  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Which of the following represents the level curves of $f(x,y)=-x-y+2?$",
  "worked_solution": "The level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equations \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nnotice that the range of $f(x,y)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ So, we will consider $c$-level curves for any real value of $c.$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ -x-y+2 & =c \\\\ y & =-x+2-c.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above gives us a family of lines with slope of $-1.$ Therefore, the correct graph is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069946/assets/e20175ef9ac473a621a67d524e60cacc6e9a40ba30267fe81b4e6a093c780c28.png)",
